abstract: >-
  Four experiments (a pilot and three studies, 6,224 trials, Claude Haiku 5.5 as a
  coding agent on tasks with hidden checks) on whether a signed, hash-chained record of the
  agent's own claims changes what it claims and what it delivers. Publishing the record showed no
  detectable effect on false completion claims. Asking for a record with evidence (instruction,
  tool and signed record together) lowered false completion by 6.6 points and raised false
  non-completion by 27.9 points; a "complete" claim was not more often true (73.5% against 77.5%)
  and the share of finished work was about the same. Pressure framing and independent acceptance
  tests showed no gain on the confirmatory tests; the pressure comparison depends on a labeller
  whose accuracy differs between arms against a rater added post hoc. Study 3's preregistered human
  audit of the completion labeller was not completed; in Study 2, a post hoc audit measured its
  agreement with two human raters on the same 80 messages. Each plan was recorded before its first
  trial. Study 3 has verified external timestamps from before its first trial (three RFC 3161 tokens and OpenTimestamps); the inspection found none for the pilot and Studies 1 and 2, whose plans carry only the signed entry times of the author's ledger (internal timestamps). Their first appearance in the inspected public ledger history is commit a7a7561 (2026-11-10 15:20:10 UTC), whose first OpenTimestamps file holds only pending attestations; the earliest verified Bitcoin attestation found is block 975319 (2026-11-10), in a later commit; both after those trials. Version 1.1.0 adds
  exploratory post hoc analyses outside the release seal; later post hoc scripts on the main branch complete that supplement and will be archived in the next release.
keywords:
  - AI agents
  - coding agents
  - false completion claims
  - study plans
  - post hoc analyses
